WEBVTT

00:00:00.546 --> 00:00:27.698
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. It's Thursday, so we're on Indigenous Lands and Innovation today. On today's episode, a Kalshi fight in U.S. courts over tribal land, where a judge just blocked sports contracts on two Rancheria lands. We've got the First Nations Major Projects Coalition and its $5.6 million BHP Foundation partnership, and whether a Nation's seat at the table is the same thing as its power to decide. Plus Bill C-39 and the consultation clock. Stay with us.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:59.893
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Thursday, November 26th, 2026. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:26.829
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we're asking whether a seat at the table is the same thing as the power to decide. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 4 degrees, with a high of 8 and light drizzle through the day. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 2 and partly cloudy, Williams Lake around 4 and partly cloudy, Quesnel around 6 under overcast, and Hanceville around 2 with overcast.

00:01:27.178 --> 00:01:28.106
<v Casey>We'll start with the news.

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:34.970
<v Casey>Most of today's news is about who gets to make the call, and a new B.C. government is making calls fast.

00:01:36.419 --> 00:02:07.361
<v Riley>Premier Lorne Doerkson's first orders since his cabinet was sworn in Friday include a return-to-office plan for the B.C. public service. Public service head Kim Henderson has been told to begin a phased return, starting with a review of existing remote work arrangements. The premier wants public servants who can work from the office back five days a week. He argues in-person work gives better service, and he frames it as fairness, since nurses, teachers and correctional workers have never had the option to stay home.

00:02:08.836 --> 00:02:20.748
<v Casey>On SOGI 123, which we flagged on Saturday, the new piece is the response. The B.C. Teachers' Federation says it's disappointed, and president Carole Gordon says the decision appears rooted in misinformation.

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:45.902
<v Riley>Quesnel's City Furniture is marking 34 years under the Wander family, with Jack and Rami taking over the location in 1992. Jack started as a delivery driver after arriving in Canada in 1979, at age 19, and worked his way up to manager. His daughter Parveen, the general manager, says the store was her playground growing up. The franchise turns 50 this year.

00:02:47.304 --> 00:03:20.422
<v Casey>The Public Service Alliance of Canada is backing Indigenous leaders and the Assembly of First Nations in calling on Ottawa to make residential school denialism a crime. The push rests on the Truth and Reconciliation Commission's 2015 final report, which covers roughly 150,000 Indigenous children forced into church-run, government-funded schools between 1857 and 1996. The commission first documented 3,200 child deaths at those schools, and the National Centre for Truth and Reconciliation says the real number could be much higher.

00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:25.040
<v Riley>The record is already in print, so the question is what a criminal law adds.

00:03:26.542 --> 00:03:50.302
<v Casey>That question of who decides runs straight into Alberta. A Yellowhead Institute brief by Michael Fabris says First Nations challenged the separatist push through strategic litigation and public mobilization, and argues that Alberta's future can't be settled without addressing Indigenous jurisdiction and treaty relationships. The brief says the fight didn't stop at the October 19th referendum ballot box.

00:03:51.856 --> 00:04:29.990
<v Riley>Robert Phillips of the First Nations Summit says repealing DRIPA, the Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples Act, could bring dire consequences in the courts, and possibly direct action on the street. Pique Newsmagazine reports he's still willing to talk with Doerkson, whom he credits with good relationships as an opposition MLA. The Globe and Mail, reporting on the mandate letters released Tuesday, says Doerkson has directed cabinet to reverse key policies on Indigenous rights, climate and substance use. Phillips opposes direct action himself, but he points to recent statements from Grand Chief Stewart Phillip.

00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:52.991
<v Casey>Earlier this week, the AFN's national chief, Cindy Woodhouse Nepinak, told the Indigenous and Northern Affairs Committee that Bill C-37, the First Nations Clean Water Act, falls short. She says it still refuses to recognize a human right to water. The earlier Bill C-61 recognized that right, but died when Parliament was prorogued, and the AFN calls C-37 significantly weaker.

00:04:54.592 --> 00:05:28.592
<v Riley>The Pirutsiagutiit framework replaces the interim Inuit Child First Initiative on April 1st, 2027. Budget 2026 proposes $575 million over five years, starting in 2027-28, to renew the program and fund the transition. The four Inuit treaty organizations will co-develop regional approaches and decide how supports are delivered inside their own treaties and self-government agreements. We covered the announcement on Thursday the 19th, so today's new detail is the money and the four regional tables.

00:05:29.042 --> 00:05:34.490
<v Casey>Four treaty organizations running their own regional delivery is a model other programs could copy.

00:05:36.032 --> 00:05:51.336
<v Riley>In Peru, the Asháninka were armed decades ago to fight Shining Path rebels. Now they're defending the Tambo River area of the Amazon against cocaine traffickers, with bows and arrows and old guns, and the Guardian reports they're doing it without outside help.

00:05:52.782 --> 00:06:33.420
<v Casey>We had the Awá approach on Thursday the 19th, so today is the response. Brazil's government has activated an emergency coordination mechanism, and the Indigenous affairs agency Funai and Indigenous health personnel are in the area. Members of the Guajajara community recorded the encounter, and Survival International shared the footage and links the visit to pressure from illegal loggers, hunters and ranchers. Local residents raised concern about thin children and older people, and about infection risk for a group with little exposure to common pathogens. Brazil's Ministry of Indigenous Peoples says its policy is to protect the Awá's right to remain isolated and to decide for themselves whether to interact.

00:06:34.959 --> 00:07:01.055
<v Riley>Denmark's parliament ratified a security agreement with Greenland and the United States on November 19th. All 109 lawmakers present voted yes. It lets the U.S. expand operations at Pituffik Space Base and set up defence areas at Narsarsuaq and Mestersvig, including for the proposed Golden Dome missile defence system. It does not need a U.S. Senate vote, and it reaffirms Greenlanders' right to self-determination.

00:07:01.505 --> 00:07:07.105
<v Casey>Self-determination is in the text. Who gets consulted on the next base expansion is the harder line.

00:07:08.529 --> 00:07:41.983
<v Riley>Squamish has declared a local state of emergency, which gives the district legal authority to carry out flood-mitigation work under B.C.'s Emergency and Disaster Management Act. Communities across the province issued evacuation alerts and closed waterfront areas as king tides, storm surge and heavy rain arrived together. Parksville and the Tla-o-qui-aht First Nation warned shoreline residents to be ready to leave. Environment Canada says tides could run 20 to 50 centimetres above the highest astronomical tides through today.

00:07:43.455 --> 00:07:59.199
<v Casey>Further down the same coast, record high tides hit San Diego, Santa Barbara and Monterey on Wednesday. Some residents were forced to flee, streets turned into creeks, and a section of a concrete pier collapsed into the ocean. The New York Times described the tides as entering uncharted territory.

00:08:00.755 --> 00:08:12.131
<v Riley>The World Court is hearing the genocide case against Myanmar, the first of its kind, over the Rohingya Muslims. The hearing runs three weeks and includes testimony on whether Myanmar committed genocide.

00:08:13.722 --> 00:08:37.434
<v Casey>A WIRED and Proof News investigation found that gig nurses booked through apps like ShiftKey often get little or no onboarding in nursing homes. ShiftKey says its network covers more than 10,000 facilities and over 200,000 health care workers. The investigation ties that gap to dangerous mistakes, including a nurse who gave a 93-year-old resident insulin meant for her roommate.

00:08:39.029 --> 00:08:55.237
<v Riley>Denmark's Supreme Court is set to rule on whether families can be forced from their homes under a law that sorts residents into "Western" and "non-Western" categories. Al Jazeera reports that evictees from estates with majority non-Western residents are fighting back.

00:08:56.844 --> 00:09:15.372
<v Casey>Twenty-five years after the arrests known as Black Tuesday, families of the Eritrea 16 still don't know whether the journalists are alive. The men were taken into state prisons in 2001. At a gathering in Nairobi, colleagues spoke about them. The families are still asking for a clear answer about what happened.

00:09:15.822 --> 00:09:22.678
<v Riley>Which brings us to who holds the pen when a Nation, a province and Ottawa disagree. First, a Community Spotlight.

00:09:24.078 --> 00:09:44.574
<v Casey>For more than twenty years, Denisiqi Services Society has provided culturally grounded child and family services for Tŝilhqot'in and Ulkatcho communities. The work keeps children connected to their own families, their own language and their own ways. Giving Tuesday falls on December 1st this year, and if you want to support it, you can find them at denisiqi dot org.

00:09:44.933 --> 00:09:48.813
<v Riley>Twenty years of service is a track record most programs never get to show.

00:09:50.213 --> 00:10:07.373
<v Riley>When a Nation sits down at a major project table, is the seat itself the power? Or is it a chair in a room where someone else writes the rules and signs the cheques? And when young people come home to hold that work, is it a duty, or a sacrifice their Nation shouldn't have to ask for?

00:10:07.703 --> 00:10:10.631
<v Casey>Depends who writes the rules, and who signs off on the money.

00:10:11.238 --> 00:10:47.628
<v Riley>The clearest rules fight right now is in the United States. Eight tribes have sued Kalshi over the past 18 months to keep its prediction markets off their lands. On November 4th, U.S. District Judge Jacqueline Scott Corley granted a preliminary injunction blocking Kalshi's sports event contracts on the lands of Blue Lake Rancheria and Chicken Ranch Rancheria of Me-Wuk Indians. That followed a September Ninth Circuit ruling that the tribes were likely to succeed under the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act. The tribes are setting terms through a federal court, and the court has so far held.

00:10:48.173 --> 00:11:02.461
<v Casey>The injunction covers tribal land. It doesn't cover the state. It protects revenue these tribes already have, and it doesn't write any new terms. The Ninth Circuit also rejected a Lanham Act claim on the contracts' legality, so the tribes depend on one statute.

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:11.974
<v Riley>Defending existing revenue is how a Nation earns the standing to negotiate new revenue. A tribe that loses its gaming base has nothing to trade.

00:11:12.652 --> 00:11:18.732
<v Casey>Earned by whom? Holding a line like that takes staff, legal budgets and people who stay in the job for years.

00:11:19.408 --> 00:11:28.592
<v Riley>Exactly. The staffing is the sovereignty. A Nation that can't pay lawyers can't hold a line, and a Nation that can't hold a line has no say over its own revenue.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:39.532
<v Casey>The injunction is a real win. The revenue question stays open, though. A court can stop a product from being sold on your land. It can't tell you what the next product should look like.

00:11:40.032 --> 00:12:10.184
<v Riley>Then the Canadian version is about getting ahead of the approval instead of reacting to it. The First Nations Major Projects Coalition has signed a partnership with the BHP Foundation worth $5.6 million, about US$3.9 million, running through March 2031. The stated purpose is to strengthen negotiations and help First Nations structure their involvement in major projects. The Logic describes the aim as helping Nations negotiate equity stakes in resource projects.

00:12:10.746 --> 00:12:20.430
<v Casey>So a mining company's foundation is paying for the bargaining strength of Nations that will negotiate with mining companies. Who is on the other side of the table when that money runs out?

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:35.366
<v Riley>Equity is the reason to take the money. A Nation with a negotiating team can ask for a stake. A Nation without one takes whatever it's offered. The coalition has 186 member Nations, and the money is aimed at closing the gap in deal readiness.

00:12:35.931 --> 00:12:44.771
<v Casey>Asking for a stake isn't a veto. The coverage describes structure and involvement. It doesn't say who holds a veto, or whose advisers are doing the modelling.

00:12:45.302 --> 00:12:55.894
<v Riley>The answer would be in the agreement. But a Nation with a stake and advisers who understand the numbers is in a better position than one reading a term sheet for the first time.

00:12:56.427 --> 00:13:20.723
<v Casey>Capacity beats none. My problem is the funder's interest. If the foundation's advisers are modelling a project's economics, the Nation has a stronger negotiator, and the mining company has a more predictable negotiation. Both can be true, and the public record doesn't tell us which one this partnership is for. The coalition is also a partner in the federal push for faster approvals, so it may be beneficiary and critic at once.

00:13:21.223 --> 00:13:38.511
<v Riley>Then the law decides whether any of it matters. A Nation with an equity stake still needs the law to let it say no or insist on terms. Bill C-39, the Building Canada Strong Act, is where that gets tested. We covered the bill on November 19th. What's new is the joint rejection.

00:13:39.194 --> 00:14:04.226
<v Casey>On October 8th, the Chiefs of Ontario, the AFN Quebec-Labrador, the BC Assembly of First Nations and Nishnawbe Aski Nation issued a joint statement titled "Rights Cannot Be Fast-Tracked." That's four regional bodies, not one youth group. The AFN has called the bill "fundamentally flawed" and wants several sections withdrawn entirely, with no room for amendments. Matawa First Nations told a House of Commons committee it's "legislative coercion."

00:14:04.752 --> 00:14:32.472
<v Riley>Withdrawn entirely is the position of people who want the bill to die. The government wants a single coordinated review and a one-year decision clock for major projects. That clock only starts once a proponent files a complete application, and it splits into a 300-day review and a 65-day decision. Projects take years to clear now, and the people who pay for that delay are the communities that were never going to be the proponent.

00:14:33.128 --> 00:14:48.584
<v Casey>Speed alone isn't the complaint. The UBCIC put it in a submission. A compressed timeline may leave the Crown unable to meet its constitutional duty to consult. That turns efficiency into a legal liability. A project approved faster and then struck down is slower in the end.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:15:08.612
<v Riley>Then write the consultation steps into the clock. A one-year decision window that includes a funded Nation review at the front is a design problem, and design problems have fixes. Officials told the committee that work with affected communities would begin well before a project reached the regulator. The bill can be held to that.

00:15:09.156 --> 00:15:29.988
<v Casey>Officials "did not expect" the bill to rush consultation. Expecting isn't a clause. During committee review, Liberal MPs rejected most of the NDP proposals to strengthen references to Indigenous rights and consent. The consent wording got voted down. The Liberals point to a proposed Crown Consultation Hub, and a hub is an office. An office can be cut in the next budget.

00:15:30.635 --> 00:15:43.923
<v Riley>The hub is still a design claim, and it's the only one on the table right now. I'd take a one-year clock if Nations are inside it at the start. Waiting has costs too. A stalled project costs a community in jobs and in years.

00:15:44.583 --> 00:15:50.183
<v Casey>The people paying for the waiting aren't in the room that sets the clock. The speed argument skips that.

00:15:50.683 --> 00:15:57.483
<v Riley>It's the strongest case against the bill as drafted. The fix is in the text, and I'd rather argue about the text than shrug.

00:15:58.036 --> 00:16:24.976
<v Casey>Then look at what happens when a Nation does push back and wins. On November 18th, B.C. Supreme Court Justice Emily Burke clarified a June ruling. The province has to engage in deep consultation with the Tsetsaut Skii km Lax Ha Nation before reconsidering whether Seabridge Gold's KSM project had "substantially started" construction in 2024. The court quashed the 2024 designation. Written submissions alone won't satisfy it, and there's no end date.

00:16:24.976 --> 00:16:45.608
<v Riley>And the environmental assessment approval is unaffected, according to Seabridge's own statement. The company says it will check with the Environmental Assessment Office on whether the ruling changes timing. So this is a reconsideration order, not a cancellation. The mine has its approval. The dispute is a procedural designation.

00:16:45.919 --> 00:16:49.407
<v Casey>The stock fell on the news. The market didn't read it as narrow.

00:16:50.047 --> 00:17:05.439
<v Riley>A stock move doesn't measure consultation. It does show that a "not yet" from a small Nation is legally real. Before the ruling, a designation could be granted without anyone asking the Nation what it thought. Now the province has to do that work again.

00:17:06.115 --> 00:17:15.691
<v Casey>And doing it again costs a company time, and costs a Nation lawyers, people and months of reading. Compress the steps in the bill and you get more of these cases, not fewer.

00:17:16.364 --> 00:17:28.052
<v Riley>The system worked here. A Nation raised a procedural defect, a court heard it, and the remedy is a redo. That's the consultation duty doing its job. The cost is real, and I'm not pretending otherwise.

00:17:28.717 --> 00:17:37.173
<v Casey>Then who pays for the Nation's side of the redo? If the answer is a project-funded negotiator, the Nation's capacity is only as long as the project's life.

00:17:37.673 --> 00:18:01.393
<v Riley>That's where the young people come in. Jeronimo Kataquapit, a youth leader with Here We Stand, put it this way: "We have to draw a line in the sand and say, 'No more.'" The December 8th day of action in Ottawa is planned by youth from Attawapiskat, and Ramon Kataquapit said it has support from Idle No More and Onion Lake First Nation. They plan to keep a presence on Parliament Hill.

00:18:02.057 --> 00:18:14.965
<v Casey>Holding a claim with a national day of action is also a load. That's unpaid political work, scheduled around a federal bill's timeline. When the consultation process needs this much mobilization, it's failing the people it was built for.

00:18:14.965 --> 00:18:34.925
<v Riley>The other reading is that the youth are doing governance work now, and people who organize a national day of action are learning how Parliament works. With the AFN, four regional bodies and the youth all opposed, the December 8th protest reads as a bargaining signal, not a fringe action. Some of them will be the staff a Nation hires later.

00:18:35.545 --> 00:18:44.753
<v Casey>Later, if the Nation can pay them. A young person who organizes a protest then goes home to a job that ends when a project closes has a career with a funding cliff in it.

00:18:45.409 --> 00:18:58.753
<v Riley>Which is why the paid position matters more than the protest. Duty versus sacrifice comes down to whether the Nation budgets for the work. Staffing a file is a job the Nation hires for and pays. That isn't a sacrifice.

00:18:59.359 --> 00:19:05.607
<v Casey>Then who's budgeting? Money for one file is not the same as money for a governance office that runs for decades.

00:19:06.196 --> 00:19:13.324
<v Riley>So the question to chase is this: do the people doing governance work sit in the Nation's own budget, or in a project's funding line?

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:17.642
<v Casey>Yes. Title settles who decides. It doesn't pay the person writing the report.

00:19:18.542 --> 00:19:41.942
<v Riley>Closer to home, the Tŝilhqot'in Nation has held Aboriginal title since the 2014 Supreme Court decision we covered on the 19th, so its own decisions about its land carry legal weight. We covered the Williams Lake First Nation and Thompson Rivers University agreement on October 29th. The same question applies there: who staffs the Nation's own priorities, and on whose timeline?

00:19:42.229 --> 00:19:50.773
<v Casey>An agreement with a university is a good thing. Who runs the programme in year five, when the agreement's first report is due and the lead staffer has a second offer?

00:19:51.092 --> 00:19:58.852
<v Riley>The answer would be in the agreement's own staffing terms. Some agreements write that in. The ones that don't are the ones to ask about.

00:19:59.415 --> 00:20:06.647
<v Casey>I'd ask the same of the Tŝilhqot'in governance work. Title makes the Nation the decision-maker. It doesn't make the staffing show up.

00:20:07.212 --> 00:20:20.708
<v Riley>We agree the Nation sets the terms. Where I part ways is on the youth. Some people want to come home, and a Nation should want them home. A governance system shouldn't force anyone to choose between their community and a career.

00:20:21.351 --> 00:20:31.903
<v Casey>Wanting them home isn't the same as needing them to carry it. A Nation that depends on its best people coming back has a succession plan that runs on hope. It works until one person leaves.

00:20:32.198 --> 00:20:36.774
<v Riley>The fix is paid positions and a budget line, not a rule that discourages return.

00:20:37.325 --> 00:20:47.829
<v Casey>I'll take the paid positions. I haven't seen a budget line in today's coverage, though. If core governance jobs sit in a project's funding line, the Nation loses its people when the project closes.

00:20:48.452 --> 00:20:59.308
<v Riley>A Nation that writes its own budget can decide whether a return is a choice or a requirement. That's what could work here, and I'd check the budget before praising any program.

00:20:59.985 --> 00:21:13.113
<v Casey>The sacrifice question is still open for me, and until I see the budget I won't call it a choice. If you have a story tip, or want to talk about staffing in your own Nation's governance work, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:17.484
<v Riley>Go easy on the drizzle out at Horsefly, and enjoy the rest of your Thursday.

00:21:18.093 --> 00:21:28.757
<v Casey>We haven't had a chance to talk directly with anyone from First Nations communications teams for today's episode, and honestly, that's a conversation we'd really like to have, so if you're in that work, reach out.

00:21:29.357 --> 00:21:48.613
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.